          {
            "title": "Read datasets/solar_event_dataset.py",
            "kind": "read",
            "raw_output": "1\timport numpy as np\n2\timport pandas as pd\n3\tfrom typing import Callable, Literal\n4\tfrom workshop_infrastructure.datasets.helio import HelioNetCDFDataset\n5\t\n6\t\n7\tclass solar_event_dataset(HelioNetCDFDataset):\n8\t    \"\"\"\n9\t    Template child class of HelioNetCDFDataset showing how to build a downstream dataset.\n10\t    Extends the base class with a flare intensity label aligned to the Surya index.\n11\t\n12\t    All ``HelioNetCDFDataset`` keyword arguments (``index_path``, ``scalers``, ``channels``,\n13\t    ``s3_cache_dir``, etc.) are accepted via ``**kwargs`` and forwarded to the base class.\n14\t    ``load_forecast_frames`` defaults to ``False`` here (flare forecasting supplies its own\n15\t    labels, so future Surya frames are never fetched); pass it explicitly to override.\n16\t\n17\t    Additional Args:\n18\t        return_surya_stack: If True (default), include the Surya image stack in the returned dict.\n19\t            Set to False to return only the flare intensity label (useful for label inspection).\n20\t        max_number_of_samples: Cap the dataset length at this value. Useful for quick experiments.\n21\t        label_transform: Optional callable applied to the ``intensity`` column of the flare index\n22\t            to produce the ``normalized_intensity`` label. Signature:\n23\t            ``(series: pd.Series) -> pd.Series``.  If ``None``, the raw intensity values are\n24\t            used as-is. Define this at the call site (e.g., in ``build_datasets()``) to keep\n25\t            normalization logic out of the dataset class.\n26\t        ds_flare_index_path: Path to the downstream flare intensity CSV index.\n27\t        ds_time_column: Column name in the flare index to use as the event timestamp.\n28\t        ds_time_tolerance: Maximum allowed time offset when matching Surya and DS indices\n29\t            (e.g., ``\"15min\"``). Unmatched entries are dropped.\n30\t        ds_match_direction: Merge direction passed to ``pd.merge_asof``. Use ``\"forward\"``\n31\t            for causal prediction (predict flares from prior solar state).\n32\t\n33\t    Raises:\n34\t        ValueError: If ``ds_flare_index_path`` is not provided, or if no overlap exists\n35\t            between the Surya and DS indices within the specified tolerance.\n36\t    \"\"\"\n37\t\n38\t    def __init__(\n39\t        self,\n40\t        # Downstream-specific parameters\n41\t        return_surya_stack: bool = True,\n42\t        max_number_of_samples: int | None = None,\n43\t        label_transform: Callable[[pd.Series], pd.Series] | None = None,\n44\t        ds_flare_index_path: str | None = None,\n45\t        ds_time_column: str | None = None,\n46\t        ds_time_tolerance: str | None = None,\n47\t        ds_match_direction: Literal[\"forward\", \"backward\", \"nearest\"] = \"forward\",\n48\t        # All HelioNetCDFDataset parameters (index_path, scalers, channels, s3_*, etc.)\n49\t        **kwargs,\n50\t    ):\n51\t        if ds_match_direction not in [\"forward\", \"backward\", \"nearest\"]:\n52\t            raise ValueError(\"ds_match_direction must be one of 'forward', 'backward', or 'nearest'\")\n53\t\n54\t        # load_forecast_frames defaults to False here: flare forecasting supplies its\n55\t        # own labels, so future Surya frames never need to be fetched from disk/S3.\n56\t        kwargs.setdefault(\"load_forecast_frames\", False)\n57\t        super().__init__(**kwargs)\n58\t\n59\t        self.return_surya_stack = return_surya_stack\n60\t\n61\t        # Load ds index and find intersection with Surya index\n62\t        if ds_flare_index_path is not None:\n63\t            self.ds_index = pd.read_csv(ds_flare_index_path)\n64\t        else:\n65\t            raise ValueError(\"ds_flare_index_path must be provided for FlareDSDataset\")\n66\t\n67\t        self.ds_index[\"ds_index\"] = pd.to_datetime(\n68\t            self.ds_index[ds_time_column]\n69\t        ).values.astype(\"datetime64[ns]\")\n70\t        self.ds_index.sort_values(\"ds_index\", inplace=True)\n71\t\n72\t        # Apply label transform if provided; otherwise use raw intensity values.\n73\t        if label_transform is not None:\n74\t            self.ds_index[\"normalized_intensity\"] = label_transform(self.ds_index[\"intensity\"])\n75\t        else:\n76\t            self.ds_index[\"normalized_intensity\"] = self.ds_index[\"intensity\"]\n77\t\n78\t        # Create Surya valid indices and find closest match to DS index\n79\t        self.df_valid_indices = pd.DataFrame(\n80\t            {\"valid_indices\": self.valid_indices}\n81\t        ).sort_values(\"valid_indices\")\n82\t        self.df_valid_indices = pd.merge_asof(\n83\t            self.df_valid_indices,\n84\t            self.ds_index,\n85\t            right_on=\"ds_index\",\n86\t            left_on=\"valid_indices\",\n87\t            direction=ds_match_direction,\n88\t        )\n89\t        # Remove duplicates keeping closest match\n90\t        self.df_valid_indices[\"index_delta\"] = np.abs(\n91\t            self.df_valid_indices[\"valid_indices\"] - self.df_valid_indices[\"ds_index\"]\n92\t        )\n93\t        self.df_valid_indices = self.df_valid_indices.sort_values(\n94\t            [\"ds_index\", \"index_delta\"]\n95\t        )\n96\t        self.df_valid_indices.drop_duplicates(\n97\t            subset=\"ds_index\", keep=\"first\", inplace=True\n98\t        )\n99\t        # Enforce a maximum time tolerance for matches\n100\t        if ds_time_tolerance is not None:\n101\t            self.df_valid_indices = self.df_valid_indices.loc[\n102\t                self.df_valid_indices[\"index_delta\"] <= pd.Timedelta(ds_time_tolerance),\n103\t                :,\n104\t            ]\n105\t            if len(self.df_valid_indices) == 0:\n106\t                raise ValueError(\"No intersection between Surya and DS indices\")\n107\t\n108\t        # Override valid indices variables to reflect matches between Surya and DS\n109\t        self.valid_indices = [\n110\t            pd.Timestamp(date) for date in self.df_valid_indices[\"valid_indices\"]\n111\t        ]\n112\t        self.adjusted_length = len(self.valid_indices)\n113\t        self.df_valid_indices.set_index(\"valid_indices\", inplace=True)\n114\t\n115\t        if max_number_of_samples is not None and max_number_of_samples < self.adjusted_length:\n116\t            self.valid_indices = self.valid_indices[:max_number_of_samples]\n117\t            self.df_valid_indices = self.df_valid_indices.iloc[:max_number_of_samples]\n118\t            self.adjusted_length = max_number_of_samples\n119\t\n120\t    def __len__(self):\n121\t        return self.adjusted_length\n122\t\n123\t    def __getitem__(self, idx: int) -> dict:\n124\t        \"\"\"\n125\t        Args:\n126\t            idx: Dataset index.\n127\t\n128\t        Returns:\n129\t            Dictionary containing:\n130\t                forecast (np.float32): Normalized log10 flare intensity label.\n131\t                ds_index (str): ISO-format timestamp from the flare index.\n132\t            When ``return_surya_stack=True``, also includes all keys from\n133\t            ``HelioNetCDFDataset.__getitem__`` (ts, time_delta_input, lead_time_delta, etc.).\n134\t        \"\"\"\n135\t        sample = super().__getitem__(idx=idx) if self.return_surya_stack else {}\n136\t        sample[\"forecast\"] = self.df_valid_indices.iloc[idx][\"normalized_intensity\"].astype(np.float32)\n137\t        sample[\"ds_index\"] = self.df_valid_indices[\"ds_index\"].iloc[idx].isoformat()\n138\t        return sample\n139\t",
            "locations": [
              "/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/solar_event/datasets/solar_event_dataset.py"
            ],
            "status": "completed",
            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_01WZQXXDLzdasXj7n5w1SJQr",
            "raw_input": {
              "file_path": "/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/solar_event/datasets/solar_event_dataset.py"
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      "time": 1789410247.8367589,
      "body": "",
      "id": "00951eb2-2d5b-4251-afe2-fc88256eee1a",
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false
    },
    {
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "This `__init__` builds `solar_event_dataset` on top of `HelioNetCDFDataset` by aligning two independent time indices \u2014 the Surya image index and a downstream flare-label index \u2014 into one matched set of valid timestamps. The downstream-specific work is everything after `super().__init__(**kwargs)`:\n\n**1. Generic vs. task-specific split (lines 38-57)**\nAll the base dataset's parameters (`index_path`, `scalers`, `channels`, `s3_*`, etc.) are absorbed into `**kwargs` and passed straight to `HelioNetCDFDataset.__init__`, matching the repo convention of never re-implementing generic dataset logic per app. It also forces `load_forecast_frames=False` by default via `kwargs.setdefault(...)`, since this downstream task supplies its own labels (flare intensity) instead of needing future Surya frames.\n\n**2. Loading the downstream label index (lines 62-76)**\n- Reads `ds_flare_index_path` (a CSV of flare events) into `self.ds_index`; raises if not provided.\n- Parses `ds_time_column` into a proper `datetime64` column `ds_index` and sorts by it.\n- Builds the label column `normalized_intensity` by applying `label_transform` (if given) to the raw `intensity` column \u2014 this keeps any normalization/log-scaling logic out of the dataset class itself, per the docstring.\n\n**3. Matching Surya timestamps to flare timestamps (lines 78-106)**\n- Takes `self.valid_indices` (the timestamps the base class already knows have complete Surya data) and asof-merges them against `ds_index` using `pd.merge_asof` with `ds_match_direction` (`\"forward\"` for causal flare prediction \u2014 predict from prior solar state).\n- Since `merge_asof` can match multiple Surya timestamps to the same flare event, it computes `index_delta` (time offset) and drops duplicates, keeping only the closest Surya/flare pair per flare event.\n- If `ds_time_tolerance` is set, discards any match whose offset exceeds it, and raises `ValueError` if nothing survives \u2014 i.e., no usable overlap between the two indices.\n\n**4. Overwriting the dataset's notion of length/indices (lines 108-118)**\nThis is the key downstream twist: it replaces `self.valid_indices` (and adds `self.adjusted_length`) with only the *matched* timestamps, so the dataset now iterates over flare-labeled samples rather than every Surya frame. `df_valid_indices` is re-indexed by timestamp so `__getitem__` can do an aligned lookup. `max_number_of_samples` optionally truncates this further for quick experiments.\n\n**Why it matters downstream:** `__len__` and `__getitem__` (lines 120-138) both rely on this adjusted index \u2014 `__getitem__` calls the parent's `__getitem__` for the Surya stack (unless `return_surya_stack=False`) and then attaches `forecast` (the label) and `ds_index` (the matched flare timestamp) from `df_valid_indices`. So the entire point of this `__init__` is turning two mismatched time series into one coherent, index-aligned dataset the Lightning training loop can iterate over.",
      "type": "msg",
      "time": 1789410261.1236494,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "id": "556d76b2-02ce-4cc6-b2fe-553643c15068"
    },
    {
      "type": "msg",
      "metadata": {
        "model": {
          "id": "sonnet",
