    {
      "id": "d596213b-3c08-4352-9a57-1e22fbc16194",
      "body": "Found it \u2014 this isn't a config mismatch, it's a mix-up between two unrelated things:\n\n- `solar_event_path` (now that you renamed the field to match the YAML) is a **field name on the `FlareDataConfig` dataclass** \u2014 a `str` attribute, not a function.\n- The notebook's import line is:\n  ```python\n  from downstream_apps.solar_event.configs import solar_event_path\n  cfg = solar_event_path(\"./configs/config_script.yaml\")\n  ```\n  It's trying to import `solar_event_path` as if it were the **loader function** and call it like `load_flare_config(...)`. But `configs.py` only exports `load_flare_config` (the `partial(load_config, data_cls=FlareDataConfig)` function) \u2014 there's no top-level `solar_event_path` name to import at all, hence the `ImportError`.\n\nYou likely renamed the dataclass field from `flare_index_path` to `solar_event_path` to match the YAML, but the notebook cell (originally copied from the template, which used `load_flare_config`) still has a leftover/typo'd variable name where `load_flare_config` should be.\n\n**Fix:** in the notebook cell, change:\n```python\nfrom downstream_apps.solar_event.configs import solar_event_path\ncfg = solar_event_path(\"./configs/config_script.yaml\")\n```\nto:\n```python\nfrom downstream_apps.solar_event.configs import load_flare_config\ncfg = load_flare_config(\"./configs/config_script.yaml\")\n```\nThe dataclass field `solar_event_path` and the loader function `load_flare_config` are two separate things \u2014 renaming the field to match your YAML key doesn't create/rename an importable function with that same name.",
      "time": 1789411624.851775,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "mentions": [],
      "type": "msg",
      "raw_time": false
    },
    {
      "raw_time": false,
